 "stories": [
  {
   "feature": "centroid_hz",
   "title": "Brightness: where the energy sits",
   "text": "Spectral centroid is the 'centre of mass' of a sound's spectrum. Rain is the brightest (2.7 kHz, broadband hiss) while helicopter is the darkest (1.2 kHz, low rotor thump). Chainsaw (2.4 kHz), crying baby (2.4 kHz) and sneezing (2.4 kHz) all land at almost the same value, so brightness alone cannot separate them. The model needs the time-frequency pattern, which is why we feed it spectrograms."
  },
  {
   "feature": "active_fraction",
   "title": "Duration: how long the sound is 'on'",
   "text": "Share of the 5 s clip within 25 dB of its loudest moment. Rain (100%), sea waves (98%), chainsaw (97%) and helicopter (97%) are continuous; sneezing (15%), dog (39%) and rooster (40%) are short bursts. Two of the three forest-threat classes (chainsaw, helicopter) are sustained, which is good news for a field sensor: a few seconds of audio is enough to catch them."
  },
  {
   "feature": "flatness",
   "title": "Texture: tonal versus noise-like",
   "text": "Spectral flatness is near 0 for pure tones and near 1 for white noise. Rooster (0.005) and dog (0.006) are tonal and harmonic; rain (0.183) is the most noise-like. Chainsaw (0.084) and sea waves (0.084) are also noise-like and, like rain, are 'always on', so the continuous noisy sounds are the group we expect to be hardest to tell apart. The confusion matrix after training tests that."
  }
 ],
 "n_clips": 400,
 "source": "ESC-10 (Piczak, 2015), 16 kHz mono, 5 s clips"
